<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avienus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Rufius Festus Avienius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avienus (Rufius Festus Avienius) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet of the 4th century CE who adapted Greek works on geography and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> into verse. His Ora Maritima, a description of the coasts of which only the part from the Atlantic to Massalia survives, preserves material from a much older Greek sailing guide and gives early information on the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p></div>
